  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protection and Reintegration of Victims of Trafficking</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The IOM, UNODC and ILO propose a two-year joint program, bringing in expertise within their mandates to improve the detection, referral, protection, return and reintegration (at national level or in the countries of origin) of VoTs. VoTs identified through this joint program can be nationals and non-nationals, adults and children, and victims of different types of exploitation. IOM will provide direct assistance, shelter, and reintegration services to VoTs through partnership agreements with two CSOs in Marrakech and Oujda which will include shelter, psychosocial counselling, legal and medical assistance, food, and non-food items, and return and reintegration activities (depending on VoTs profiles) in agriculture, transports, and education etc.  UNODC will focus on strengthening the identification, protection of VoTs during the prosecution, investigation and adjudication phase, as a continuation of previous activities undertaken by the agency, and ILO will complement IOM&#x2019;s reintegration services through trainings to institutional actors and will focus on capacity building activities to the National Mutual Aid of Morocco and the National Agency for the Promotion of Employment and Skills (ANAPEC), to strengthen coordination of accompanying measures towards skilling and employment of VoTs as well as on socioeconomic inclusion through prior learning recognition, improved skills, and social enterprise development. The three agencies will work jointly to provide capacity building activities and trainings that will strengthen partnerships between the judiciary, CSOs, institutional partners, the National Mutual Aid for Morocco, and ANAPEC to reinforce the protection and reintegration of VoTs into communities. Vision Statement: The project&#x2019;s vision is to further identify, protect and socio-economically reintegrate VoTs into communities, while reinforcing partnerships and knowledge on TiP.Project Goal: The project aims for the improved protection and well-being of VoTs through a national system that is more efficient, and victim centered. Victims feel safe and protected, can recover from the trauma of trafficking, and are socio-economically integrated into communities. Project Objectives: IOM Objective 1: Morocco reinforces the detection, identification, protection, return and reintegration of VoTs through CSOs and IOM&#x2019;s Assisted Voluntary and Reintegration Program, in addition to reinforcing knowledge on TiP and coordination and cooperation among stakeholders. Outcome 1.1: VoTs are identified, protected, and reintegrated into communities. Output 1.1: Improved access to tailored services, shelter, and reintegration. Activity 1.1.1: Direct identification, assistance, protection, and shelter of 90 VoTs through grants to two CSOs in Marrakech and Oujda and the reintegration of 62/90 VoTs in Morocco over a two-year period. Activity 1.1.2: Assisted voluntary return of 28/90VoTs to their countries of origin (Moroccan VoTs returning to Morocco and non-national VoTs returning to their countries of origin), in addition to the reintegration of 15/90 non-national VoTs in their countries of origin.Outcome 1.2: Increase in the number of VoTs identified and referred for protection services. Output 1.2: Improve skills and knowledge of CSOs staff, responsible authorities, frontline workers, and project staff to identify protect and reintegrate VoTs into their communities. Activity 1.2.1: Conduct 1 trainings of trainers to 10 selected CSOs staff and institutional actors (certified by the NC, UNODC and IOM) to ensure the continuity of 2 additional trainings to CSOs staff and institutional actors (90 participants in total) on the identification, referral, protection, and assistance of VoTs in different Moroccan regions. This activity will be conducted jointly with UNODC and it further complements UNODC&#x2019;s activities 2.3.1 and 2.3.2 on the capacity building of CSOs. Activity 1.2.2: Organize exchange activities/trainings for staff, government officers and/or CSOs on the thematic of counter trafficking (CT) to build on best practices implemented in diff</narrative>
  </description>
